# Day 12 – Generative AI Learning Journey
**Date:** 27 July 2026

## What I did today
- Reviewed a bakery DPR and cross-checked machinery prices against live market sources
- Built a case-specific authenticity-check framework, then a generic DPR analysis metaprompt
- Critiqued the framework and upgraded it to v2 (promoter check, vendor authenticity, outlier handling, severity, residual risk)
- Applied v2 to the Rajneesh Bebni DPR and produced a structured Word review
- Drafted professional bank mails for a GST/account identity-theft complaint (branch reply + RO escalation)
- Created a printable A4 landscape class timetable PDF
- Reviewed HRO/aviation-style reliability ideas for strengthening regulatory frameworks

## What I learned
- Every quoted asset must pass both price plausibility and process relevance
- A milk analyzer in a bakery DPR is a process mismatch, not just a price issue
- Generic frameworks must start with sector identification, then apply sector-specific checks
- Self-critique before locking a framework improves quality more than polishing prose
- Professional bank correspondence should state what was already checked and what access is still needed

## One small win
- Produced a reusable DPR analysis metaprompt and applied it successfully to a real case

## Tomorrow’s focus
- Lock DPR v2 as a usable skill/checklist and test it on one more real DPR
- Avoid opening a new abstraction layer until v2 has been used on 2–3 live cases
